   455	### 7.1 Streaming Entries (assistant, thinking)
   456	
   457	```text
   458	entry_upsert  -->  entry_delta (0..N)  -->  entry_commit
   459	   |                    |                       |
   460	   |  isStreaming=true  |  content grows        |  isStreaming=false
   461	   |  content=""        |  via concatenation    |  content is final
   462	```
   463	
   464	1. The server sends `entry_upsert` with `isStreaming: true` and an empty `content`.
   465	2. The server sends zero or more `entry_delta` messages. The client MUST concatenate each `delta` to the entry's `content`.
   466	3. The server sends `entry_commit`. The client MUST set `isStreaming` to `false`, matching the committed entry the server keeps in the session. No further deltas will arrive for this entry ([validated by: leaves the committed assistant entry in the session, no longer streaming](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L105)).
   467	4. Every entry the server opens is committed by the time the turn ends, on every terminal path: a `stop`, a round that ends without one, and a provider that throws mid-stream. The partial content is committed exactly as streamed, and on a throw the `entry_commit` frames arrive before the `error` frame ([validated by: commits a partial answer as streamed, before the error, when the provider throws](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L414), [validated by: commits the answer, flagged truncated, when a round ends on a tool call with no stop chunk](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L449)).
   468	5. An entry committed because the run ended before it finished (a provider failure, or a round with no `stop`) carries `truncated: true`, in the session and on the wire: the server re-sends it as an `entry_upsert` with the flag just before its `entry_commit`, so a live client and a replay of the stored entry both see it, and a client can render a cut-off answer as cut off without the transient `error` frame. An entry committed on a `stop` never carries the field, and a suppressed entry is flagged in the session but never re-sent. Any text the thinking-tag parser still held is flushed into the entry first, as a `stop` does ([validated by: flags a partial answer truncated in the session and re-sends it with the flag before its commit](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L488), [validated by: leaves an entry committed on a stop chunk without the flag and sends it once](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L516), [validated by: flags a suppressed entry that a throw cut short in the session but never re-sends it](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L528), [validated by: keeps the text the thinking-tag parser still held when a throw cuts the answer short](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L540)).
   469	
   470	### 7.2 Non-Streaming Entries (user, tool)
   471	
   472	```text
   473	entry_upsert
   474	   |
   475	   |  Complete on arrival.
   476	   |  No delta or commit follows.
   477	```
   478	
   479	The entry is fully formed in the `entry_upsert` message, so a tool call yields one `entry_upsert` and nothing further. The client MUST NOT expect `entry_delta` or `entry_commit` for these entries ([validated by: sends a tool entry for a tool call](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L161)).
   480	
   481	## 8. Conversation Sequence
   482	
   483	A typical conversation produces entries in this order:
   484	
   485	```text
   486	Index 0: UserEntry         (user sends message)
   487	Index 1: ThinkingEntry     (AI reasoning, streamed)
   488	Index 2: ToolEntry         (AI invokes tool)
   489	Index 3: AssistantEntry    (AI response, streamed)
   490	```
   491	
   492	In multi-tool scenarios, multiple ToolEntry objects may appear between the ThinkingEntry and AssistantEntry. The tool execution loop (Section 9) may produce additional entries.
   493	
   494	- A reply with no tool calls and no thinking produces, in order, the user entry, the assistant entry opened empty, one `entry_delta` per text chunk, its `entry_commit`, and `stream_end` ([validated by: sends the user entry, then the assistant entry, its delta, its commit and stream_end](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L91)).
   495	- A thinking block is committed before the assistant entry that follows it is opened, so the two never interleave ([validated by: commits the thinking entry before the assistant entry opens](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L117)).
   496	
   497	### 8.1 Example: Full Conversation Exchange
   498	
   499	```json
   500	<-- {"type": "connected", "sessionId": "550e8400-...", "message": "Connected to HAL Engine", "examplePrompts": ["..."]}
   501	
   502	--> {"type": "user_message", "content": "What is the weather in Berlin?"}
   503	
   504	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 0, "entry": {"role": "user", "content": "What is the weather in Berlin?", "timestamp": "2026-01-15T14:30:00Z"}}
   505	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 1, "entry": {"role": "thinking", "content": "", "isStreaming": true}}
   506	<-- {"type": "entry_delta",  "index": 1, "delta": "I should look up the weather for Berlin."}
   507	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 2, "entry": {"role": "tool", "toolName": "get_weather", "toolInput": {"location": "Berlin"}}}
   508	<-- {"type": "entry_commit", "index": 1}
   509	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 3, "entry": {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "timestamp": "2026-01-15T14:30:02Z", "isStreaming": true}}
   510	<-- {"type": "entry_delta",  "index": 3, "delta": "Berlin currently has "}
   511	<-- {"type": "entry_delta",  "index": 3, "delta": "a temperature of 18 degrees Celsius with clear skies."}
   512	<-- {"type": "entry_commit", "index": 3}
   513	<-- {"type": "stream_end"}
   514	```
   515	
   516	Notation: `-->` is client-to-server, `<--` is server-to-client.
   517	
   518	**Commit frames are not emitted in index order.** Above, `entry_commit` for index 1 arrives after `entry_upsert` for index 2. A thinking entry is committed when the next text segment arrives, not when a tool entry appears, so a tool call between thinking and the answer lands in the gap. A client that assumes commits arrive in ascending index order, or that an entry is committed before the next one opens, will mis-render this exchange ([validated by: commits the thinking entry when text resumes, not when a tool entry appears](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L134)).
   519	
   520	### 8.2 Example: Tool with Suppression and Index Skip
   521	
   522	When a tool suppresses the AI response, the server sends `entry_skip` for entries that exist in the session but are not forwarded to the client. The client must adjust subsequent indices accordingly.
   523	
   524	```json
   525	--> {"type": "user_message", "content": "Convert 100 miles to km"}
   526	
   527	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 0, "entry": {"role": "user", ...}}
   528	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 1, "entry": {"role": "tool", "toolName": "convert_units", ...}}
   529	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 2, "entry": {"role": "assistant", "content": "**100** miles = **160.93** kilometers", ...}}
   530	<-- {"type": "entry_skip",   "index": 3}
   531	<-- {"type": "stream_end"}
   532	```
   533	
   534	Here, server index 3 is the suppressed AI echo. The client tracks index 3 as skipped. When the user asks a follow-up:
   535	
   536	```json
   537	--> {"type": "user_message", "content": "Explain the result"}
   538	
   539	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 4, "entry": {"role": "user", ...}}
   540	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 5, "entry": {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "isStreaming": true, ...}}
   541	<-- {"type": "entry_delta",  "index": 5, "delta": "The conversion means..."}
   542	<-- {"type": "entry_commit", "index": 5}
   543	<-- {"type": "stream_end"}
   544	```
   545	
   546	The client adjusts: server index 4 becomes client index 3, server index 5 becomes client index 4. The entries land at the correct array positions.
   547	
   548	### 8.3 Example: Suppression After the Model Has Already Spoken
   549	
   550	A model often narrates before it calls a tool. That narration is streamed to the client as it arrives, so by the time the tool asks for suppression the client is already displaying it. It cannot be skipped; it is retracted.
   551	
   552	```json
   553	--> {"type": "user_message", "content": "Convert 100 miles to km"}
   554	
   555	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 0, "entry": {"role": "user", ...}}
   556	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 1, "entry": {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "isStreaming": true, ...}}
   557	<-- {"type": "entry_delta",  "index": 1, "delta": "Let me convert that for you."}
   558	<-- {"type": "entry_commit", "index": 1}
   559	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 2, "entry": {"role": "tool", "toolName": "convert_units", ...}}
   560	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 3, "entry": {"role": "assistant", "content": "**100** miles = **160.93** kilometers", ...}}
   561	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 1, "entry": {"role": "assistant", "content": "", ...}}
   562	<-- {"type": "entry_skip",   "index": 4}
   563	<-- {"type": "stream_end"}
   564	```
   565	
   566	The second `entry_upsert` at index 1 is the retraction: same index, empty content. Index 1 is NOT tracked as skipped -- it still occupies its slot in the client array. Index 4, the model's suppressed follow-up, is.
   567	
   568	### 8.4 Example: Error During Streaming
   569	
   570	A provider fails after the answer has started. The partial answer is re-sent flagged `truncated` and committed as streamed, then the client receives the `error` frame, then `stream_end` as the last frame of the run, and clears its processing state on `stream_end`, not on `error`.
   571	
   572	```json
   573	--> {"type": "user_message", "content": "Where is my booking?"}
   574	
   575	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 0, "entry": {"role": "user", ...}}
   576	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 1, "entry": {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "isStreaming": true, ...}}
   577	<-- {"type": "entry_delta",  "index": 1, "delta": "Your booking is "}
   578	<-- {"type": "entry_upsert", "index": 1, "entry": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Your booking is ", "isStreaming": true, "truncated": true, ...}}
   579	<-- {"type": "entry_commit", "index": 1}
   580	<-- {"type": "error",        "code": "SERVER_ERROR", "message": "Failed to process message"}
   581	<-- {"type": "stream_end"}
   582	```
   583	
   584	## 9. Tool Execution Loop
   585	
   586	When the AI model requests a tool invocation, the server executes the tool and re-queries the model with the result. The loop is bounded by `maxToolRounds` (default 5): at most `maxToolRounds` tool rounds are executed, and the provider is called at most `maxToolRounds + 1` times, so the default permits six model calls: the first, plus five more that each read a round's tool results. A tool round the model requests once the budget is spent is not executed; the turn ends on that call's output. No new frame type is introduced for this outcome: a consumer's `onToolBudgetExhausted` sentence arrives as an ordinary assistant entry (`entry_upsert`, `entry_delta`, `entry_commit`), and the turn still ends with `stream_end`, never `error` ([validated by: renders the hook's sentence as its own committed entry, then stream_end, with no error](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L571)).
   587	
   588	```text
   589	Round 1:  Model streams text + requests tool_use
   590	          Server yields ToolEntry via entry_upsert
   591	          Server executes tool
   592	          Server re-queries model with tool result
   593	
   594	Round 2:  Model streams text (possibly requests another tool)
   595	          ...
   596	
   597	Round N:  Model streams final text with stopReason "end_turn"
   598	          Server commits all open entries
   599	          Server sends stream_end
   600	```
   601	
   602	The client observes this as a sequence of `entry_upsert`, `entry_delta`, and `entry_commit` messages, terminated by a `stream_end`. The tool loop is transparent to the client -- it does not need to track rounds.
   603	
   604	Messages a tool addresses to the client are forwarded verbatim, in the position the stream produced them, rather than being rewritten into entries of the server's own ([validated by: forwards a tool result to the client untouched](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L169)).
   605	
   606	## 10. Heartbeat
   607	
   608	Two levels of heartbeat operate concurrently.
   609	
   610	### 10.1 WebSocket-Level Ping (Server-Initiated)
